#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158136} :activity/id #uuid "924e2aff-70c8-5662-9585-30e32759e61f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049711 :topic/id #uuid "0d1e3652-33dd-5422-ac25-674cfeb4e737" :topic/math-academy-id 2833} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158484 :tutorial/content "![](images/34/34fdc5325fe4b1fb73d203ccb9db81e5d8b4b1cb02b3efda2b928addc2e458e6.png)\n\nSuppose that the area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sin(5\\theta)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the **total area** bounded by the curve? \n\nOne method is to count the number of petals and multiply the area of one petal by the total number of petals. In our case, we have $5$ petals in total, and so the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total}=5A =  \\dfrac{5}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ceb97ae-7dd4-5b54-b6f0-ccfa1ff9c1c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4441 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "46bff2e6-5534-59d5-8395-e50507c10b20" :step/math-academy-id 27331 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158137}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157295 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157099 :question/id #uuid "98bc19b4-ab9d-51e3-9190-965bdc787845" :question/math-academy-id "e-7366"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c0740-2003-5c99-84f2-294c88d57ba7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049612}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544896}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "6274289a-17a6-5a8c-aebd-822328b86ef8" :step/math-academy-id 27332 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158138}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158138 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157296 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157100 :question/id #uuid "5caa3fbc-f232-5e40-b6d2-c34fa552d6b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7367"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09043c6b-599d-5c5d-ac4b-20dd6275a8f9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050080}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544868}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "c2f89fc1-eb29-50ee-92a1-d9365468ebdd" :step/math-academy-id 27333 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157101 :question/id #uuid "a994968f-db5b-57f7-b836-f3ec295bfd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-7368"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544843}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "34f635d8-7098-582d-a722-326a66beefae" :step/math-academy-id 27334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158485 :tutorial/content "![](images/34/34fdc5325fe4b1fb73d203ccb9db81e5d8b4b1cb02b3efda2b928addc2e458e6.png)\n\nLet's go back to our polar curve $r=\\sin(5\\theta).$ Recall that the area bounded by one petal is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nCan we find an expression for the **total area** bounded by the curve by varying the limits of integration only?\n\nIndeed, we can, and we do this by extending the upper bound of the integral to include all $5$ loops. \n\nNote that:\n\n- The $1$st petal is traversed from $\\theta_1=0$ to $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5},$ and the length of this interval is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}.$\n\n- To find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nContinuing this method, we obtain the following intervals for all $5$ petals of our rose:\n\n![](images/81/814d2ff9a4ca7e8c7e6d85cc374eff37034e541338705c2abf621e5fd82f90e0.png)\n\n![](images/6c/6cc39d6b6fdf29c3b582f867ea9e769b5c6bf466bc57d53a3f346e092375b89c.svg)\n\nNote that the curve traces the petals out of order. However, all $5$ petals are traced out as $\\theta$ ranges from $0$ to $\\pi.$\n\nTherefore, we can obtain the total area of the given polar curve if we traverse it from $\\theta = 0$ to $\\theta = \\pi \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It might be tempting to extend the top bound to $2\\pi.$ However, the entire curve is traced out on the interval $\\theta\\in [0,\\pi],$ so integrating from $0$ to $2\\pi$ would give us *twice* the total area bounded by the curve!" :tutorial/id #uuid "be01da54-00a0-5ce1-8112-30c4e3bd1ded" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4455 :tutorial/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "386f572e-8a18-54a0-b592-6c5f0e677c8f" :step/math-academy-id 27417 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158139}}] :activity/title "The Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157489}]}
